  "live_records": {
    "q-128448": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128448",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x-y}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{2}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4 & =x-y \\\\ y & =x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the line $y=x-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
