    "q-128450": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128450",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(-2,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,-1) & =4{(-2)}^{2}+9{(-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4(4)+9(1) \\\\  & =16+9 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the $25$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=25.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
