    "q-71201": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71201",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $M(2,3)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}-15.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $M$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3) & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2(3)^{2}-15=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $M(2,3)$ is the $5$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}-15 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $M(2,3)$ is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{y}^{2}=20.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
