    "q-36829": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36829",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=-1$ gives a line\n\nII. $c=0$ gives a parabola and the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$\n\nIII. $c=1$ gives two lines",
      "worked_solution": "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x & =c \\\\ xy({x}^{2}-y) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=\\pm 1,$ which are not lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the parabola $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nSo, only statement II is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
