    "q-35534": {
      "dom_id": "step-q35534",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a hyperbola\n\nII. $c=2$ gives a parabola\n\nIII. $c=3$ gives an ellipse",
      "worked_solution": "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ (c-2){x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
