    "q-71413": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71413",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14135461/assets/29253192bb8863030c3464dd2c42be40922db96f3de6f3e57674f2a59f862e09.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =c \\\\ x & =\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that in the equations $x=\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14135461/assets/c5ea7e325483eeb3572c90b78a5cf72040d84b98dee51a1ca2bdf4dcab5b8d2f.png)",
